{"kind":"assert","text":"Decision on `27579850`: a ref is a value. C4 keeps no private cut\nbehind a ref object, and it throws no error for a copied ref.\n\nReason. A page copies its values. A card passes them across a frame\nby structured clone. A store or a developer tool serializes a model.\nAn association by object identity is lost in each case, and the\nauthor cannot see why. A contract that holds only for the original\nobject is a trap for the first two consumers, the cards and\ndap-foldkit.\n\nThe rule, in one sentence: the basis of a subject is read when the\ndraft first names that subject.\n\n1. A subject that the slot names by a ref takes `seen` from the ref\n   value, as the page gave it. A copy of a ref is that ref. If the\n   row moved after the page read it, the basis is stale, and the\n   submit returns the draft with `now`. That is the intended result.\n2. A subject that the act derives (`also.via`, `also.by`, `one`)\n   takes its revision from the client's checked view at the edit\n   that first determines it, through `expectedOf`. For most acts\n   that is the first edit of the empty slot, where the intention\n   starts (client note, section 2 rule 1). A field that selects a\n   subject later captures that subject at that later edit. A change\n   of the selecting field captures again for that subject only.\n3. The draft store holds the captured basis from that write. A\n   later render, a new slot value and a reload change nothing in it.\n   A stored basis that is missing or damaged is not repaired from\n   current rows. The draft stops `shape`.\n4. A slot is an address: the kind and the item names. Two slots with\n   equal names are one slot. A slot holds no cut.\n5. A page that shows a frozen copy of a row opens its draft when the\n   dialog opens, with `edit(slot, {})`. The how-to page says so. No\n   new call.\n6. A ref whose name the view does not hold stops `state`. A subject\n   that a field selects and the view does not hold gets no basis.\n   The submit sends no guessed revision, and the scope judges the\n   act. Tell me if `expectedOf` cannot leave that key out.\n7. Accepted from your proposal: the return takes `now` and the\n   revisions of its derived subjects from the cut of the exploration\n   that decided the movement, inside the Replica operation. C4 never\n   reads a later `client.view` for it. The public `ActRef` keeps its\n   three fields.\n\nThe limit, which the C4 note states: a derived subject can move\nbetween the page's read and the edit that names it, and C4 does not\nsee that movement. The primary subject has no such gap, because its\n`seen` comes from the page. Your case of P at revision 8 and A at\nrevisions 2 and 3 is this limit when the page edits after H+1. Rule\n5 closes it for a page that opens the draft at H.\n\nIf the source shows a case where this rule signs an act that the\nmember did not see the basis of, beyond that stated limit, send me\nthe case before code.","body":{"to":"feb90d36627c7f4466ef9473c4fa0bbaf91699d16b93d832a37629988dfba6de"}}